---
/**
 * Both sides fetching ONE GitHub Actions failure log.
 *
 * WHAT THIS SECTION ACTUALLY MEASURED, AND WHY THE CONCLUSION IS THE OPPOSITE OF
 * THE ONE THIS PAGE USED TO MAKE
 *
 * The job is: find the failing run, find the failed job, read its log.
 *
 * The payload is enormous and neither side controls it. GitHub's runs endpoint returns
 * 35 fields per run, of which `repository` and `head_repository` are the same nested
 * repo object twice — 7,684 of 10,814 bytes — plus two full user objects. Three runs
 * cost 12,573 tokens to learn eight useful things, which is 224 tokens of useful
 * things.
 *
 * That dwarfs everything this section used to argue about. MCP's resident definitions
 * are 2,877 tokens on 14 tools; against a single 12,573-token response, residency is
 * noise, and the 48x / 77x ratios computed from it were measuring the wrong term of
 * the equation.
 *
 * AND THE RAW ENDPOINT FAVOURS MCP HERE
 *
 * `f gh GET` forwards the request and prints the body. It has no projection — a --jq
 * flag is accepted and ignored, which is worse than rejecting it. So a model using `f`
 * must pipe through jq, which means `f` requires TWO tools: bash and jq. The MCP side
 * needs one exec tool, which is one schema plus a shell pipeline.
 *
 * This is the strongest argument against this page's thesis and it is stated in the
 * headline rather than buried.
 *
 * WHAT THE MCP SERVER CANNOT DO AT ALL
 *
 * @modelcontextprotocol/server-github is the canonical reference server. tools/list
 * returns 26 tools — repositories, issues, PRs, commits, reviews, code search. There is
 * NO tool for workflow runs, jobs, logs or artifacts: actionsTools is an empty array.
 * So an MCP-only agent cannot complete this task with its declared capabilities and
 * must shell out regardless. The comparison below is therefore "server + exec" against
 * "`f` + jq", which is what an agent actually ends up with in both cases.
 *
 * Earlier revisions used @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem for this. It cannot
 * fetch a log either — it only sees a root directory — so those numbers described a
 * job nobody was doing.
 *
 * WHY THIS IS THE ARGUMENT FOR `f` ANYWAY
 *
 * Not because `f` is cheaper here — it is not, on this workload. Because a uck is
 * editable source: `f gh` can grow a projection in the repo that owns it. A tool list
 * is whatever upstream shipped, and a missing capability means waiting on someone
 * else's roadmap or bolting on a shell.
 *
 * The text that scrolls is reconstructed; every token figure is measured, with
 * scripts/measure-gh-log.mjs, against live GitHub API responses from fclidev/fcli and
 * a live tools/list from the server. o200k_base.
 */

// scripts/measure-gh-log.mjs — run: npm run measure:gh
const MCP = {
  server: "@modelcontextprotocol/server-github",
  tools: 26,
  resident: 3546,
  actionsTools: 0,
  execCall: 65,
};

const F = {
  bashDef: 44,
  jqDef: 61,
  index: 57,
};

// The payloads both sides must swallow, measured from live responses.
const PAYLOAD = {
  runs: { tokens: 12573, bytes: 45368, useful: 224, fields: 35 },
  jobs: { tokens: 1721, bytes: 5383 },
  logs: { tokens: 10570, bytes: 23769 },
};
const payloadTotal = PAYLOAD.runs.tokens + PAYLOAD.jobs.tokens + PAYLOAD.logs.tokens;

const mcpTotal = MCP.resident * 3 + MCP.execCall + payloadTotal;
const fTotal = (F.bashDef + F.jqDef + F.index) * 3 + payloadTotal;

const pct = (a, b) => Math.round((a / b) * 100);
---

<div class="compare">
  <p class="compare-job">
    One job, both ways: find the failing Actions run, find its failed job, read the log.
    Counters are cumulative token payload, measured against live GitHub API responses.
    The scrolling text is a realistic reconstruction, not a recording.
  </p>

  <div class="compare-panes">
    <section class="compare-col" aria-label="Simulated session using the GitHub MCP server">
      <p class="compare-head">
        <span class="compare-svc">MCP</span>
        <span class="compare-sub">{MCP.tools} tools + exec</span>
        <span class="compare-count" data-count-for="mcp" data-count-to={mcpTotal}><b>{mcpTotal.toLocaleString()}</b> tokens</span>
      </p>
      <div class="stream" data-stream="mcp">
        <div class="turn">
          <p class="turn-h">tools resident <span>{" "}{MCP.resident.toLocaleString()}</span></p>
          <pre class="ln body">tools: get_file_contents, search_repositories,
       list_commits, get_pull_request, create_issue, …
       <em>26 tools · 0 that reach Actions</em></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="turn">
          <p class="turn-h">turn 1 <span>{" "}{(MCP.resident * 2 + PAYLOAD.runs.tokens).toLocaleString()}</span></p>
          <pre class="ln asst">{`{ "tool_use": "exec", "input": { "command":
  "curl -s $API/actions/runs | jq -c '[.workflow_runs[]
   | {name, head_branch, status, conclusion}]'" } }`}</pre>
          <pre class="ln tool">{"[fcli] main success\n[fcli] main success\n[ci] main failure"}</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="turn">
          <p class="turn-h">turn 2 <span>{" "}{mcpTotal.toLocaleString()}</span></p>
          <pre class="ln asst">{`{ "tool_use": "exec", "input": { "command":
  "curl -s $API/actions/runs/39148294696/jobs | jq …" } }`}</pre>
          <pre class="ln tool">{"build  failure\nlint   success"}</pre>
          <pre class="ln asst">{`{ "tool_use": "exec", "input": { "command":
  "curl -sL $API/actions/jobs/117736555994/logs | gunzip
   | tail -400" } }`}</pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="compare-note">
        No declared tool covers Actions — {" "}
        <code>actions/list_runs</code> would have to exist. The agent shells out anyway,
        so the server's {MCP.resident.toLocaleString()} tokens buy a capability this job
        does not use.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="compare-col" aria-label="Simulated session using f">
      <p class="compare-head">
        <span class="compare-svc f">f</span>
        <span class="compare-sub">2 tools + index</span>
        <span class="compare-count" data-count-for="f" data-count-to={fTotal}><b>{fTotal.toLocaleString()}</b> tokens</span>
      </p>
      <div class="stream" data-stream="f">
        <div class="turn">
          <p class="turn-h">tools resident <span>{" "}{F.bashDef + F.jqDef + F.index}</span></p>
          <pre class="ln body">tools: bash, jq
$ f
config creds df fj gh ha providers search uck</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="turn">
          <p class="turn-h">turn 1 <span>{" "}{((F.bashDef + F.jqDef + F.index) * 2 + PAYLOAD.runs.tokens).toLocaleString()}</span></p>
          <pre class="ln asst">{`{ "tool": "bash", "input": { "command":
  "f gh GET /repos/fclidev/fcli/actions/runs?per_page=3" } }`}</pre>
          <pre class="ln tool">
<em>{PAYLOAD.runs.tokens.toLocaleString()} tokens come back.</em> The runs endpoint
returns {PAYLOAD.runs.fields} fields per run — <code>repository</code> and
<code>head_repository</code> are the same object twice.</pre>
        </div>
        <div class="turn">
          <p class="turn-h">turn 2 <span>{" "}{fTotal.toLocaleString()}</span></p>
          <pre class="ln asst">{`{ "tool": "bash", "input": { "command":
  "f gh GET …/actions/runs/39148294696/jobs" } }`}</pre>
          <pre class="ln tool">{PAYLOAD.jobs.tokens.toLocaleString()} more.</pre>
          <pre class="ln asst">{`{ "tool": "bash", "input": { "command":
  "f gh GET …/actions/jobs/117736555994/logs" } }`}</pre>
          <pre class="ln tool">{PAYLOAD.logs.tokens.toLocaleString()} more.</pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="compare-note">
        <code>f gh</code> forwards and prints. It has no projection — a <code>--jq</code>{" "}
        flag is accepted and ignored — so the model must pipe, and <code>jq</code> has to
        be a tool.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <table class="cache-table">
    <caption>
      Where the tokens actually went, both sides. Payload is what GitHub returned;
      overhead is definitions and discovery, counted across 3 turns.
    </caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Term</th>
        <th scope="col">MCP</th>
        <th scope="col">f</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">API payload</th>
        <td>{payloadTotal.toLocaleString()}</td>
        <td>{payloadTotal.toLocaleString()}</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Definitions + index × 3</th>
        <td>{(MCP.resident * 3).toLocaleString()}</td>
        <td>{((F.bashDef + F.jqDef + F.index) * 3).toLocaleString()}</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Total</th>
        <td>{mcpTotal.toLocaleString()}</td>
        <td>{fTotal.toLocaleString()}</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p class="compare-verdict">
    The payload is <b>{pct(payloadTotal, mcpTotal)}%</b> of the{" "}
    <b>{mcpTotal.toLocaleString()}</b> an MCP session spends, and{" "}
    <b>{pct(payloadTotal, fTotal)}%</b> of the <b>{fTotal.toLocaleString()}</b> that{" "}
    <code>f</code> spends. Discovery is the remainder, and on this job it barely
    registers. What <em>does</em> register: <code>f gh</code> cannot project, so the
    model pays {PAYLOAD.runs.tokens.toLocaleString()} tokens for the{" "}
    {PAYLOAD.runs.useful.toLocaleString()} tokens of run metadata it needed. A uck is
    editable source — that projection is a change to one file here — while a tool list
    is whatever upstream shipped, and the canonical GitHub server has{" "}
    <b>no Actions tool at all</b>.{" "}
    <a href="/docs/measurements/">how these were measured</a>
  </p>
</div>
